WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.870
We might know about viruses because
they can infect us and cause diseases

2
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.850
like the flu, herpes and smallpox. In
fact, viruses come in all shapes and

3
00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:14.560
sizes and can affect all life forms on
Earth, including animals, plants, and

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.450
even bacteria.

5
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.320
Viruses are everywhere on the planet.
In fact, one teaspoon of seawater has

6
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.600
10 million viruses in it.

7
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.430
But what exactly is a virus? Viruses
are made of an outer shell that holds

8
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:33.860
genetic material. This genetic material
is the recipe for viruses to make more

9
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:38.150
viruses, but viruses don't have the
tools to make more of themselves.

10
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.470
They need to borrow tools from another
life form, or host. Viruses infect

11
00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.910
cells, the basic unit of life, which
have all the tools the virus needs to

12
00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.520
copy itself. To get at the cell's
tools, the virus first needs to put its

13
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.260
genetic material inside of the host
cell. Once the genetic material is

14
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:03.690
inside, viruses can then immediately
make more copies of themselves which

15
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:08.230
are then released into the environment,
resulting in the death of the host

16
00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.270
cell. Other times the virus chooses to
hide and wait until the host is

17
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:17.660
stressed and weakened before making
more copies of itself. Host cells, like

18
00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:21.180
bacteria, have evolved many types of
defenses against viruses.

19
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.190
For example, bacteria have evolved
protein scissors to tear apart the

20
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.850
genetic material of viruses as soon as
they enter the cell. Studying such

21
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:35.270
tools has helped many fields of
research, like genome editing. While we

22
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:39.330
tend to only think of viruses is bad,
viruses can also be helpful in the

23
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:39.950
environment.

24
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:45.600
For example, some viruses in the ocean
recycle nutrients by killing their host

25
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.250
cells, which provides food for other
organisms.

26
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.050
Viruses can kill us, but without them
we wouldn't be here.

